 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Embroidery requires a stitch file that differs from ordinary logo artwork. The production partner converts the art into instructions for its embroidery equipment. That conversion affects the finished appearance.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 That conversion is called <em>digitizing</em>. It requires judgment about stitch direction, density, underlay, and fabric. Review the file and proof for every approved garment program.
 </p>

 <h2 id="what-it-is" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">1. What Digitizing Actually Is</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 An embroidery machine doesn't read a JPG, a PDF, or even a vector AI file. It reads a <em>stitch file</em> — typically a `.dst`, `.emb`, `.exp`, or `.pes` format depending on the machine. The stitch file tells the machine: drop the needle here, then here, then here, this color thread, this stitch type, this density.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Digitizing is the process of converting your logo into that stitch file. A digitizer (a person, not a script) decides:
 </p>

 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li>What stitch type to use for each section (satin, fill, run)</li>
 <li>Stitch density and direction</li>
 <li>The order operations run in (so the design doesn't pucker or warp)</li>
 <li>Underlay (foundation stitches that stabilize the fabric)</li>
 <li>Where the machine starts and ends</li>
 <li>How to handle small details, type, and gradients</li>
 </ul>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 A simple-looking logo can still require many stitch decisions. Ask how the selected partner reviews small type, stitch direction, density, and the intended garment.
 </p>

 <h2 id="why-matters" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">2. Why a Bad File Haunts Every Reorder</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Here's the trap. Your first order with a new shop comes back, and the logo looks "fine." Not great, but fine. The lettering is a little uneven, the fill stitch on the icon has a gap on one side, the polo puckers slightly under the embroidery. You shrug, take the order, move on.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 A reorder that reuses the same uncorrected file can repeat the same issue. Review the garment, placement, and proof before each production approval. File changes and fees depend on the partner agreement.
 </p>

 <div class="my-10 border-l-4 border-gold-500 bg-gold-500/5 p-6">
 <p class="mb-2 font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">The compounding cost</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 A poor digitizing file can create repeated problems. A strong file gives the production partner a better starting point. Each reorder still needs a current garment and proof review.
 </p>
 </div>

 <h2 id="what-to-send" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">3. What to Send Your Embroidery Shop</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 The single best thing you can do is send a <strong>vector logo file</strong> — `.ai`, `.eps`, or `.svg`. Vectors scale infinitely without quality loss, which means the digitizer can size your logo to the exact embroidery dimensions without guessing.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 If you only have a JPG or PNG, send the highest-resolution version available. The production partner will confirm whether it can use the file or needs new artwork.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Also send:
 </p>

 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Pantone colors</strong> for every part of the logo (PMS coated and uncoated if you have both)</li>
 <li><strong>The intended placement and size</strong>, confirmed against the exact garment, artwork, and approved proof</li>
 <li><strong>The fabric</strong> we'll be embroidering on (canvas vs polyester pique vs FR fabric all behave differently)</li>
 <li><strong>Any "do not touch" rules</strong> — minimum logo widths, brand colors that can't shift, type that must remain readable</li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="what-good-looks-like" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">4. What a Good Digitizing File Looks Like (When You See It on a Shirt)</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 You'll know good digitizing by what you don't see. A well-digitized logo has:
 </p>

 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Clean satin stitch edges</strong> — no fraying, no gaps where two colors meet</li>
 <li><strong>Even fill density</strong> — the center of a filled shape doesn't look thin or patchy</li>
 <li><strong>Type that reads clearly</strong> at the actual size, with no clumped or broken letters</li>
 <li><strong>No fabric puckering</strong> around the embroidery — the shirt sits flat</li>
 <li><strong>Crisp registration</strong> — colors line up exactly where they should, no overlap or gap</li>
 </ul>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Bad digitizing has the opposite — uneven edges, thin fill, illegible small text, fabric that bunches under the embroidery, and colors that don't quite line up. Some of those are a press operator issue. Most of them trace back to the file.
 </p>

 <h2 id="sew-out" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">5. Confirm the Sew-Out Process Before Production</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 A production partner may sew the digitized logo onto a test garment before the full order. This is the sew-out. Confirm the proof process, approval steps, and any sample cost in your quote.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 The sew-out catches issues you'd never see on a digitizer's screen — a logo that looks perfect on the simulation but puckers on actual canvas, type that's legible at 4" but blurs at 3", thread color that shifted slightly on the fabric. We'd rather find that on one shirt than on a hundred.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Confirm whether the quote includes a sew-out, production sample, or digital proof. Put the approval process and any sample cost in writing.
 </p>

 <h2 id="owning" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">6. Who Owns the File?</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Important question, often missed in the first conversation. Some shops digitize at their cost, sew at their cost, and consider the stitch file their property. If you ever want to leave that shop, you start over.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Ask for file ownership, delivery, storage, and reuse terms in writing. Bighorn confirms those terms in the quote for the selected production partner.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Worth confirming with whatever shop you work with, before they have your logo on file.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
